# DURABLE REPRO -- the `.get()()` sibling of the non-identifier-callee class.
# A `Vector[Callable[void(int &)]].get(0)(&a)` call (or the analogous
# `Dict.get(k)(&a)`) SEGFAULTs on both C and LLVM. Track K's
# `infer_collection_element_type` fix retires the direct-subscript cells
# (`arr[0](&a)`, `d["k"](&a)`) but NOT this one: `.get()` returns via a
# METHOD-CALL result temp whose type comes from method-signature inference,
# not from the collection-element-type path Track K teaches to consult
# `callable_alias_sigs`.
#
# Measured 2026-07-28 (post-Track-K on the fix branch):
#   Vector[Callable[void(int &)]].get(0)(&a)   -> SEGV exit 139 on C AND LLVM
#   Dict[String, Callable[void(int &)]].get(k)(&a) -> SEGV exit 139 on C AND LLVM
# Both shapes hit the same underlying gap; this fixture pins the Vector shape
# as the representative -- the Dict shape shares the same fix.
#
# Fix direction: teach the method-call result-type inference to preserve the
# full FnPtr sig (params + ownerships + return) when the receiver is a
# `Vector[Callable[...]]` or `Dict[K, Callable[...]]` and the method is
# `.get()`. The `callable_alias_sigs` side-table Track K added is the
# consumer that already knows how to build a full-sig FnPtr -- the
# method-inference path needs to route through it.
#
# INTENDED (asserted): 101. Same reference-grade semantics as the
# graduated `callable_amp_arr_indexed_callee.gg` sibling.
#
# AXIS / CELL: non-identifier-callee axis, METHOD-CALL-RESULT cell.
void bump(int &n):
    n = n + 100

void main():
    int a = 1
    Vector[Callable[void(int &)]] arr = []
    arr.push(bump)
    arr.get(0)(&a)
    print(a)
